==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}

===Etymology===
{{m|la||haru-|intestines}} + {{m|la||-spex}}, from {{m|la|speciō||to observe, watch}}.<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref> Compare {{cog|xfa|𐌇𐌀𐌓𐌉𐌔𐌐(𐌄𐌗)}}.

According to Nocentini<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref> the first part stems from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰer-||intestine}}, whence also Latin {{m|la|hariolus}}, {{m|la|hernia||hernia}}, and it is cognate to Ancient Greek {{m|grc|χορδή}}, Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*garnō||intestines}} (whence German {{m|de|Garn}}) and to Lithuanian {{m|lt|žarnà||intestine}}.

The second part of ''haru-spex'' can also be found in the Latin word {{m|la|auspex}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|haruspex}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|haruspex|haruspicis|m|third}}

# [[diviner]] who reads from the intestines of sacrificial animals; one who practices [[haruspicy]].

